    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <p>
        Labor Day is more than a day off. It is a living testament to the power of working people organizing together — and a reminder that the struggle for justice continues.
      </p>

      <h2>The Roots of Labor Day</h2>

      <p>
        Labor Day was born from the efforts of the labor movement in the late 19th century. Workers fought for eight-hour days, safe working conditions, and the right to organize. Many of these workers — immigrants, Black workers, women — were pushed to the margins, yet they built the unions and movements that gave us the weekend, child labor laws, and workplace protections.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>"The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles." — Karl Marx &amp; Friedrich Engels</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The first Labor Day was celebrated on September 5, 1882, in New York City, organized by the Central Labor Union. By 1894, it had become a federal holiday. But the fight was far from over.
      </p>

      <h2>Victories Won by Organized Labor</h2>

      <p>
        The gains won by the labor movement are foundational to American life:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>The 40-hour work week</li>
        <li>Minimum wage laws</li>
        <li>Workplace safety regulations</li>
        <li>Health benefits tied to employment</li>
        <li>The right to collectively bargain</li>
        <li>Child labor prohibitions</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        None of these were gifts from benevolent employers. They were won through strikes, boycotts, picket lines, and sometimes martyrdom. The workers who won these rights did so knowing they might be blacklisted, beaten, or even killed.
      </p>

      <h2>Battles Still Ahead</h2>

      <p>
        Today, the labor movement faces new challenges. Union density has declined. Gig workers lack basic protections. Wages have stagnated while productivity has soared. The wealth gap between the top one percent and everyone else has reached levels unseen since the Gilded Age.
      </p>

      <p>
        Recent years have seen a labor resurgence — from Starbucks and Amazon unionization efforts to massive strikes by UAW, UPS Teamsters, and auto workers. These victories show that when workers organize and stand together, they can win.
      </p>

      <h2>Why DSA Cares About Labor</h2>

      <p>
        Democratic Socialists of America has always been intertwined with the labor movement. We believe that workers should own and control the workplaces they labor in. We advocate for democratic economic planning, not markets left to their own devices. And we stand in solidarity with every worker on strike, every union being formed, and every fight for justice.
      </p>

      <p>
        The High Peaks chapter of DSA supports local labor struggles, attends picket lines, and builds bridges between our movement and the working class. Because socialism is not an abstract theory — it is the practical project of working people taking power over their own lives.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Get Involved</h2>

      <p>
        If you want to honor Labor Day the right way — by joining the fight — here are some ways to start:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Join or support your local union</li>
        <li>Attend DSA High Peaks meetings</li>
        <li>Show up on picket lines</li>
        <li>Organize your own workplace</li>
        <li>Vote for candidates who stand with workers</li>
        <li>Amplify worker voices in your community</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Solidarity forever.
      </p>
